<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barbados</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Bridgetown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Easternmost Caribbean island, independent since 1966 and a parliamentary republic since 2021, with one of the region's oldest parliaments.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>English settlers arrived in 1627, and Barbados became the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and slave society of the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-assembly-of-barbados">House of Assembly</a> dating from 1639. After labour riots in 1937 and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grantley-adams">Grantley Adams's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados-labour-party">Barbados Labour Party</a>, it achieved self-government, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/errol-barrow">Errol Barrow</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-labour-party-barbados">Democratic Labour Party</a> it became independent on 30 November 1966.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For 55 years Barbados was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth</a> realm with the British monarch as head of state. Power alternated peacefully between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-labour-party-barbados">Democratic Labour Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados-labour-party">Barbados Labour Party</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/errol-barrow">Barrow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tom-adams">Tom Adams</a> invested in education and health, and the economy shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> to tourism and offshore financial services, giving the island one of the highest living standards in the region.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>On 30 November 2021 Barbados became a parliamentary republic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandra-mason">Sandra Mason</a> as the first president and the singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rihanna">Rihanna</a> named a national hero. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mia-mottley">Mia Mottley</a>, in office since 2018, has promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgetown-initiative">Bridgetown Initiative</a> on climate finance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barbados.jpg" alt="Barbados" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User:Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Barbados.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bridgetown</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Errol Barrow, Tom Adams, Mia Mottley</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism and other Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Bajan Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/barbados">Barbados</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=barbados" aria-label="Barbados on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3045 1690 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barbados lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3365 1946L3365 1946"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3365 1946h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3365 1946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3365 1946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bridgetown. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=barbados">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
